<h2>The dispute beneath the award</h2>
<p>The contractor undertook construction of a road over bridge at Raichur under a 2011 agreement. Completion slipped — on the arbitrator&rsquo;s findings, because of late mark-out and design changes attributable to the employer — leading to a supplementary agreement in 2014 and an extension of time granted in July 2015 without penalty. A sole arbitrator, appointed by the High Court under Section 11, allowed six of nine claims by an award of 23 November 2021: the final bill, price adjustment, idling of machinery and labour, and overheads computed on the Emden formula.</p>
<p>The employer&rsquo;s Section 34 petition failed before the Commercial Court, Bengaluru. The Section 37 appeal gathered every available objection — jurisdiction under Section 16, limitation, denial of cross-examination, and a head-by-head attack on the claims — and failed on all of them.</p>

<h2>How each objection fell</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Jurisdiction.</strong> The appointment was court-made under Section 11, and the appellant participated without protest. A Section 16 objection must be raised before the tribunal at the proper stage; it cannot be stored for appeal.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Limitation.</strong> The extension of 7 July 2015 without penalty, coupled with unsettled final accounts, kept the claims alive. Limitation in running construction accounts turns on when the employer&rsquo;s position crystallised — not on the bare completion date.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Procedure.</strong> The cross-examination grievance found no traction where the record showed the parties&rsquo; cases were put and met on documents the tribunal evaluated.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Merits.</strong> Each allowed claim was a plausible, contract-based conclusion. Plausibility is the ceiling of review: neither perversity nor patent illegality was shown, and nothing less unlocks interference.</div>
</div>

<h2>The precedential spine</h2>
<p>The Bench assembled the modern canon of appellate restraint: <em>MMTC v. Vedanta</em> on the derivative nature of Section 37 review; <em>Konkan Railway v. Chenab Bridge</em> and <em>UHL Power</em> on the prohibition against merits reassessment; <em>Larsen Air Conditioning</em> and <em>Delhi Airport Metro Express</em> on the confined meaning of patent illegality; and the recent reaffirmations in <em>Sanman Rice Mills</em> and <em>Somdatt Builders</em>. The through-line is institutional: the arbitrator is the chosen judge of fact and contract; Section 34 is a safety valve, not an appeal; and Section 37 reviews the Section 34 court&rsquo;s discipline, not the award afresh.</p>

<h2>What employers and contractors should take away</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Raise Section 16 early or lose it.</strong> Participation without protest before a court-appointed arbitrator forecloses the jurisdictional ground for all later stages.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Extensions have consequences.</strong> An extension granted without penalty is an admission about responsibility for delay — and it reshapes both limitation and the merits of delay claims.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Formula-based claims hold.</strong> Overheads and idling computed on recognised formulae, anchored in proved delay, are arbitrator&rsquo;s territory that review courts will not re-compute.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Budget for finality.</strong> Two tiers of challenge consumed five years after the award without changing a rupee. Parties pricing litigation risk after an adverse award should price exactly that.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">For Delhi practice the framework is identical: Section 34 petitions in works-contract matters go to the Commercial Courts or the High Court&rsquo;s Commercial Division by value, and Section 37 appeals to the Division Bench apply the same restraint — recently restated by the Supreme Court in the SEPCO line — without re-opening facts.</div>
